    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Privacy</strong></td>
      <td>100% client-side. Your PDF never touches a server</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>File size limit</strong></td>
      <td>Depends on browser memory — typically 50-100 MB</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Best for</strong></td>
      <td>Quick conversions of single PDF files, text-heavy documents</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Limitations</strong></td>
      <td>Complex layouts (multi-column, heavy images) may need desktop tools</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>To use this method, open your browser, navigate to the converter, select your PDF, and click convert. The resulting EPUB will include reflowable text, basic formatting, and embedded images. This is the best starting point for most users.</p>

<h2 id="method-2-calibre">Method 2: Calibre — The Swiss Army Knife</h2>

<p><strong>Calibre</strong> is the most powerful free ebook management tool available. Its built-in EPUB converter handles PDF input natively and gives you fine-grained control over the output.</p>

<h3 id="calibre-steps">Step-by-step with Calibre</h3>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Install Calibre</strong> — available for Windows, macOS, and Linux. Download the latest version from calibre-ebook.com (currently 9.11 as of July 2026).</li>
  <li><strong>Add your PDF</strong> — click "Add books" and select your PDF. Calibre imports it into your library.</li>
  <li><strong>Convert</strong> — select the imported PDF and click "Convert books" → "Convert individually."</li>
  <li><strong>Configure output</strong> — in the EPUB output section, adjust these settings:
    <ul>
      <li>Enable <strong>"Heuristic Processing"</strong> for better paragraph detection</li>
      <li>Set <strong>"Line Unwrap Factor"</strong> to 1.0 for most PDFs (higher for multi-column)</li>
      <li>Enable <strong>"Insert blank lines between paragraphs"</strong> if the output looks cramped</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Click OK</strong> — Calibre processes the PDF and produces an EPUB in your library.</li>
  <li><strong>Verify</strong> — right-click the EPUB and select "View" to preview before transferring to your device.</li>
</ol>

<p>Calibre's PDF converter is especially good at preserving headings, bold text, and italics. It struggles with complex layouts — PDFs that have multiple columns, floating images, or tables — but for standard text documents, it produces excellent results.</p>

<h2 id="method-3-pandoc">Method 3: Pandoc — Best for Technical Users</h2>

<p><strong>Pandoc</strong> is the command-line tool of choice for anyone comfortable with a terminal. It supports virtually every document format and gives you precise control over the conversion process.</p>

<p>Installation is straightforward:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>macOS:</strong> <code>brew install pandoc</code></li>
  <li><strong>Linux:</strong> <code>sudo apt install pandoc</code> (or <code>sudo dnf install pandoc</code>)</li>
  <li><strong>Windows:</strong> download the installer from pandoc.org</li>
</ul>

<p>Once installed, the basic command is:</p>

<pre><code>pandoc input.pdf -o output.epub</code></pre>

<p>Pandoc uses pdfinfo (from the poppler-utils package) and pdftotext to extract text from the PDF. For better results with structured documents, add these options:</p>

<pre><code>pandoc input.pdf -o output.epub \
  --toc \
  --metadata title="Your Book Title" \
  --metadata author="Author Name" \
  --epub-cover-image=cover.jpg</code></pre>

<p>Pandoc also supports conversion to EPUB from other intermediate formats. A common power-user pipeline is:</p>

<pre><code># PDF → Markdown → EPUB (better control over formatting)
pandoc input.pdf -o temp.md
# Edit temp.md if needed
pandoc temp.md -o output.epub --toc</code></pre>

<p>Pandoc gives you the best results for PDFs with consistent heading structures (academic papers, technical reports, books). It fares poorly with scanned PDFs or image-heavy layouts.</p>

<p>If you are converting Markdown sources directly rather than PDFs, see our <a href="/blog/markitdown-vs-pandoc-2026/">Markitdown vs Pandoc 2026 comparison</a> for a side-by-side benchmark of the two most popular Markdown-to-document pipelines.</p>

<h2 id="method-4-sigil-import">Method 4: Sigil — Manual EPUB Editing with PDF Import</h2>

<p><strong>Sigil</strong> is the leading free WYSIWYG EPUB editor. While it is primarily a tool for editing EPUB files, it can also import content from PDF through a multi-step workflow that gives you maximum control.</p>

<p>The process works like this:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Copy text from your PDF (using any PDF reader's select tool).</li>
  <li>Open Sigil and create a new EPUB project (<strong>File → New</strong>).</li>
  <li>Paste the copied text into Sigil's editor view.</li>
  <li>Use Sigil's <strong>Tools → Clean Source → Format HTML</strong> to clean up formatting.</li>
  <li>Add chapter breaks using <strong>Insert → Special Character → Page Break</strong>.</li>
  <li>Generate a table of contents from your headings (<strong>Tools → Table of Contents → Generate from Headings</strong>).</li>
  <li>Save the EPUB.</li>
</ol>

<p>Sigil is the best choice when automatic conversion produces bad results and you need to fix the EPUB manually. It is also the best tool for reflowing scanned PDFs, since no automated tool handles OCR-to-EPUB reliably — you will need to copy text section by section anyway.</p>

<p>The latest version (Sigil 2.8, covered in our <a href="/blog/sigil-2-8-image-editing-epub-guide-2026/">Sigil 2.8 guide</a>) adds the ImageTab plugin that makes it easier to handle images extracted from PDFs during manual EPUB construction.</p>

<h2 id="method-5-kindle-create">Method 5: Kindle Create (for Amazon Publishing)</h2>

<p>If you are converting a PDF to EPUB specifically for self-publishing on Amazon KDP, <strong>Kindle Create</strong> is worth mentioning. As of July 2026, Kindle Create natively exports EPUB (Amazon now requires EPUB as the submission format starting September 1, 2026).</p>

<p>Kindle Create imports PDF and DOCX files and gives you a visual editor to adjust formatting, add drop caps, and set up chapter breaks. Once your file looks right, export it as EPUB and submit directly to KDP.</p>

<p>This method works best for books with simple layouts and consistent chapter structure. It is less suitable for academic papers, technical manuals, or any document with complex tables and figures.</p>

<p>For a full comparison of self-publishing tools, see our <a href="/blog/calibre-vs-sigil-2026/">Calibre vs Sigil 2026</a> guide.</p>

