<h2 id="common-pitfalls">Common PDF-to-EPUB Pitfalls</h2>

<p>No matter which method you choose, watch out for these common issues:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Scanned PDFs</strong> — A PDF that is just a collection of scanned images contains no text. You need OCR (Optical Character Recognition) first. Free options: Google Docs (upload PDF, it OCRs automatically), or <a href="https://pandoc.org">Pandoc</a> with Tesseract. After OCR, export as EPUB.</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-column layouts</strong> — Two-column PDFs often result in EPUBs where the text of both columns is mixed together. Calibre's heuristic processing partially fixes this. For perfect results, you may need to convert column by column manually.</li>
  <li><strong>Missing images</strong> — Some converters strip embedded images. Both Calibre and Pandoc preserve them, but online tools vary. Check the output before deleting your original PDF.</li>
  <li><strong>Encoding issues</strong> — PDFs with special characters (em dashes, accented letters, mathematical symbols) may not convert correctly. Calibre's heuristic processing handles most of these, but Sigil is your fallback for manual correction.</li>
  <li><strong>Table of contents</strong> — Most PDFs do not have a semantic table of contents. Your EPUB will need one added manually or via Calibre's TOC generation tools.</li>
</ul>

